      <h2 id="evening">One Tuesday night</h2>

      <p>Twenty-two people, four courts, 7:30pm. Court 2 finishes. Someone arrived at 8:05 and has not played yet. One of your regulars has just played three on the trot and wants to sit the next one out. Two players have partnered each other twice already.</p>

      <p>On a peg board, you hold all of that in your head while four people wait and look at you.</p>

      <p>ePegboard puts the next game on the screen the moment the court frees up, with the players it has chosen and how even it expects the game to be. You can take it, swap somebody out, or ignore it entirely and build the game yourself.</p>

      <div class="shot" style="max-width:290px;margin:22px auto">
        <img src="/Images/Next_game_sample.jpg" alt="ePegboard's next game card: two pairs, an Excellent match-quality badge, an expected 21-21 score, and Swap, Clear and Auto pick controls">
      </div>

      <p style="text-align:center;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.9rem;margin-top:-6px">The suggestion, how close it expects the game to be, and the controls to overrule it.</p>

      <p style="margin-top:22px">The late arrival gets picked up because waiting time counts. The player who wants a rest is paused with one tap and comes back in when they say so. The pair who keep being drawn together are steered apart because recent partners count against a pick. <strong>None of that needs you to remember it.</strong></p>

      <h2 id="what">What it changes about your evening</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Games are closer.</strong> The picker balances the four players so the score lands nearer 21-19 than 21-8. Everybody gets a game worth playing rather than a formality.</li>
        <li><strong>Sitting out is shared out.</strong> Waiting time is part of the decision, so the same three people do not end up carrying the evening's rests.</li>
        <li><strong>You stop being the referee.</strong> When the pick is on a screen and the reasoning is visible, "why am I not on?" stops being a question about your judgement.</li>
        <li><strong>You get to play.</strong> That is the actual point. An organiser who is not running the board all night is an organiser who is on court.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Practical things that matter on the night: no player accounts are needed, a shared tablet or laptop can run the whole session, late arrivals and early leavers are ordinary rather than disruptive, and you can always overrule the software.</p>

      <h2 id="evidence">Whether clubs actually stay</h2>

      <p>The honest test of any of this is not a feature list, it is whether clubs run a second night.</p>

      <p><strong>Two in three clubs that ran a real first session with ePegboard this year ran another, and getting on for half have run five or more.</strong> Measured on 9 September 2026, counting only sessions in which a game was actually started, and excluding demo and template clubs.</p>

      <p>Separately, we commissioned AI evaluations of thirty club-night products against a rubric written before anything was scored. ePegboard came first in all three runs. We have <a href="/compare">published all of it</a> - the prompts, the scores, the products we could not assess, our own weaknesses, and the places a rival beats us.</p>

      <h2 id="shortlist">The shortlist</h2>

      <p>Eight products a UK club could adopt for a badminton club night today, that publish enough for these claims to be checked. There are more than eight in the market; these are the ones with real UK relevance and readable documentation.</p>

