      <h2 id="different">Live queue or planned rounds?</h2>

      <p>Before comparing ratings or leaderboards, decide whether the software should plan the evening <strong>as it happens</strong> or <strong>as a sequence of rounds</strong>. It is the decision most comparisons skip and it rules out half the market either way.</p>

      <p>A live system waits for a court to finish, looks at who is available now and chooses the next game. It absorbs late arrivals, early departures and courts finishing at different times. A round-based system generates the whole evening up front: legible, printable, and constantly edited by reality.</p>

      <p>Neither is better. A stable nine-player group may prefer knowing all five games at 7pm. A twenty-plus club with arrivals until 8:15 almost certainly wants live decisions. <strong>ePegboard, ShuttlePilot, Court Manager, ShuttlSync, ShuttleStats, NextFour and QourtX are live; SportRevive plans rounds.</strong></p>

      <p>The second question is worth asking any vendor: <strong>what can outweigh queue order?</strong> Skill, partner repeats, a requested rest? Ask them to explain a case where the longest-waiting player is not picked. The answer tells you how the product actually behaves at 9pm.</p>

      <h2 id="alternatives">When another product suits you better</h2>

      <p>Genuinely, and with the reason:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>ShuttleStats</strong> - if chasing session fees is a bigger drain than choosing games. Members book and pay through Stripe, with capacity, waitlists and refunds attached, and there is no subscription: the club pays a flat fee per member payment. ePegboard records what was paid but does not collect it.</li>
        <li><strong>Court Manager</strong> - if your hall's Wi-Fi is unreliable. It runs on the device and keeps working without a connection, and it specifically handles venue login pages interfering with that. Our web app needs a connection; our fully offline version is a separate Windows product.</li>
        <li><strong>ShuttlePilot</strong> - if the live night is the whole job and you want it run one-handed from a phone while you play. It is the only product we found that equalises sit-outs <em>across sessions</em> rather than only tonight.</li>
        <li><strong>SportRevive</strong> - if your numbers are stable and you would rather see the whole evening in advance, printed. Only the organiser needs an account.</li>
        <li><strong>NextFour</strong> - if you want the pegboard metaphor kept exactly as your club knows it, on phones, with a gradual move rather than a change of approach.</li>
        <li><strong>ShuttlSync</strong> - if seasons, achievements and social stats matter as much as the night itself, and four courts is your ceiling.</li>
        <li><strong>QourtX</strong> - if you are a multi-sport venue wanting membership and operations around the session rather than only game rotation.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 style="margin-top:26px">A note on QourtX's pricing</h3>
      <p>Worth one extra check before a committee budgets for it: its badminton page and its main pricing page describe different commercial models - a member-band monthly price on one, a per-active-member tier on the other. That may be a transition in progress. Ask for the applicable plan in writing.</p>

      <h2 id="limits">What ePegboard does not do</h2>

      <p>Taken from the same evaluations we published, including the parts that do not suit us:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>It does not take money.</strong> Payments are a record of what was collected, not a card machine. Cash, cheque or transfer, typed in afterwards.</li>
        <li><strong>The web app needs a connection.</strong> Fully offline running means our separate Windows desktop product, at GBP 60 a year.</li>
        <li><strong>Competitions are handicap doubles.</strong> Singles and team formats are not built.</li>
        <li><strong>We publish the picker's signals but not their weights</strong>, so you cannot tune the balance between a close game and a long wait yourself.</li>
        <li><strong>We suggest a tablet or laptop</strong> rather than running the night from a phone.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="test">If you want to test it properly</h2>

      <p>Do not test with eight well-behaved imaginary players. Run a real night and make it awkward: a late arrival after several games, someone sitting out and rejoining, two courts finishing together, a pairing you want kept, a suggestion you overrule, and a wrong score that needs correcting.</p>

      <p>Then ask the one question that matters: <strong>did this take decisions out of my head, or just move them onto a screen?</strong></p>

      <p>Also test leaving. Export whatever the product says belongs to your club. A system can be free to enter and expensive to leave if years of results cannot come with you.</p>

